WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.611
In plain code, an exception has an easy path.

2
00:00:05.611 --> 00:00:13.349
A function throws, and the exception climbs back up to whoever called it, one caller at a time, until someone catches it.

3
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:16.604
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

4
00:00:16.604 --> 00:00:19.738
A launched coroutine has no caller waiting for it.

5
00:00:19.738 --> 00:00:22.433
The line that started it moved on long ago.

6
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:25.379
So when it throws, where does the exception go?

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.816
The answer is a picture we've been building.

8
00:00:31.816 --> 00:00:34.759
A dinner has courses, and a course has dishes.

9
00:00:34.759 --> 00:00:43.719
The dinner is the root coroutine, the main course is a coroutineScope inside it, and the roast, the potatoes and the gravy are its children.

10
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:48.954
Every coroutine becomes a child of the scope it starts in, so the work forms a tree.

11
00:00:48.954 --> 00:00:54.163
Its first rule you know already: a parent can't finish until all of its children have.

12
00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:55.688
Now the roast burns.

13
00:00:55.688 --> 00:01:00.905
At 100 milliseconds it throws BurnedException, an exception the demo made up.

14
00:01:00.905 --> 00:01:03.547
The potatoes would need a whole second.

15
00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:05.241
Should they keep cooking?

16
00:01:05.241 --> 00:01:07.273
And when does the caller hear?

17
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:12.523
Here's what the tree does, in order.

18
00:01:12.523 --> 00:01:17.024
One: the roast fails, and reports the exception to its parent, the course.

19
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.338
Two: the course is cancelled with that exception, and a cancelled job cancels all of its children.

20
00:01:23.338 --> 00:01:29.288
The potatoes and the gravy come off the heat, and their finally blocks, the clean up code, run.

21
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:32.439
Three: the course waits until they have really stopped.

22
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:38.930
Four: coroutineScope throws the BurnedException to its caller, who can catch it like any exception.

23
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.912
The family hears what happened.

24
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:49.514
So one failure takes down the whole course, and comes out as one exception, where the work started, after about 100 milliseconds.

25
00:01:49.514 --> 00:01:51.085
Nothing is left cooking.

26
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:56.172
The lesson's FailureSpreads runs this with a roast and potatoes.

27
00:01:56.172 --> 00:01:58.662
The roast burned at 161 milliseconds.

28
00:01:58.662 --> 00:02:04.851
The potatoes' finally block ran 2 milliseconds later, and the caller caught it 2 after that.

29
00:02:04.851 --> 00:02:06.869
Potatoes: done, never printed.

30
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:08.735
Why 161, and not 100?

31
00:02:08.735 --> 00:02:14.139
A JVM, the program that runs the code, is slow just after it starts.

32
00:02:14.139 --> 00:02:16.602
It was still finding its apron.

33
00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:19.701
That part is the machine, not the tree.

34
00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:28.566
Many of us carry a picture from Java's executors, the pools that run tasks you hand them: every task is on its own.

35
00:02:28.566 --> 00:02:36.032
On an executor, the roast fails, and the potatoes carry on to potatoes: done, at about 1,000 milliseconds.

36
00:02:36.032 --> 00:02:37.511
close waits for them.

37
00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:42.547
The roast is on fire, and the potatoes are still being lovingly peeled.

38
00:02:42.547 --> 00:02:45.985
So the point is: in Java, you cancel them by hand.

39
00:02:45.985 --> 00:02:49.972
With that one line, their finally ran at 198 milliseconds.

40
00:02:49.972 --> 00:02:52.584
coroutineScope does that step for you.

41
00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:55.526
Now the other direction.

42
00:02:55.526 --> 00:02:58.081
The guests phone, and call the dinner off.

43
00:02:58.081 --> 00:03:00.696
That's a cancel, and it comes from the top.

44
00:03:00.696 --> 00:03:05.805
It travels down: the dinner is cancelled, then the course, then every dish below it.

45
00:03:06.733 --> 00:03:07.966
And the other way?

46
00:03:07.966 --> 00:03:10.158
Say only the gravy is cancelled.

47
00:03:10.158 --> 00:03:11.596
Does the course stop?

48
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:14.811
No.

49
00:03:14.811 --> 00:03:16.429
A cancel never goes up.

50
00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:26.983
A child that's cancelled, or that ends by throwing CancellationException, the exception a cancelled coroutine throws, counts as cancelled, not failed.

51
00:03:26.983 --> 00:03:31.416
Its parent isn't told, and the roast and the potatoes carry on.

52
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:42.623
This is structured concurrency: work that runs at the same time has the same nested shape as the code that started it, and failures and cancels follow that shape.

53
00:03:42.623 --> 00:03:45.840
A failure goes up the tree and stops the siblings.

54
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.156
A cancel goes down, and stops there.

55
00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:55.752
This is the default because it's almost always what you want: if one dish is ruined, the course is off.

56
00:03:55.752 --> 00:04:03.151
Sometimes you do want siblings to survive, like a reminder sent by text, email and phone, where any one is enough.

57
00:04:03.151 --> 00:04:05.812
Lesson 6 brings in a supervisor for that.

58
00:04:06.533 --> 00:04:09.170
And here the picture breaks, on timing.

59
00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.807
A real cook pulls the pans off at once.

60
00:04:11.807 --> 00:04:20.055
A coroutine hears the cancel only at its next suspension point, so a long calculation, or a blocking call, finishes first.

61
00:04:20.055 --> 00:04:22.219
Lesson 5 shows how to cooperate.

62
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.102
Here's the demo's core in Kotlin, shortened, with the Java executor beside it.

63
00:04:28.102 --> 00:04:31.372
In Kotlin, the scope cancels the potatoes for you.

64
00:04:31.372 --> 00:04:33.857
In Java, you write that line yourself.

65
00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.070
So, where does a child's exception go?

66
00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.825
Up the tree, to its parent.

67
00:04:38.825 --> 00:04:45.261
The parent stops the siblings, waits for them, and hands one exception to whoever started the work.

68
00:04:45.261 --> 00:04:47.341
A cancel only ever travels down.

69
00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:48.330
Quick check.

70
00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.190
Nobody calls off the dinner.

71
00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.448
Someone cancels just the potatoes.

72
00:04:52.448 --> 00:04:55.901
What happens to the roast, the gravy and the course?

73
00:04:58.933 --> 00:05:00.586
Nothing changes for them.

74
00:05:00.586 --> 00:05:05.212
The potatoes count as cancelled, not failed, so the course isn't told.

75
00:05:05.212 --> 00:05:07.657
The roast and the gravy keep cooking.
